    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\n`membershipTier` now returns `GOLD` when the owner's household (owners sharing the same `householdId`) has 3+ members after the create; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply.\n\n## Files changed (4)\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `householdMemberCount` field + getter and a small `assignHouseholdMemberCount(existingOwners)` method that counts how many existing owners share this owner's `householdId`, plus one for itself. I invoke it from the end of the already-existing `assignNamesakeCount(...)` \u2014 both are creation-time counts derived from the same existing-owners collection.\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `membershipTier` now returns `\"GOLD\"` when `householdMemberCount >= 3`, before falling through to the unchanged SILVER/BRONZE logic.\n\n3. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `household_member_count INTEGER` column (hsqldb is the test profile; `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must be declared).\n\n4. **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 appended a trailing `NULL` to the 10 seed `owners` inserts to match the new column count.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\nThe key lever (per the cost model) is avoiding edits to methods inside large classes. The natural-looking spot would be `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`, but that class carries a large `WMC_other`. Instead I hooked the new count into the small `Owner` entity's existing create-time counting path \u2014 which the service *already* calls with the existing-owners collection \u2014 so **no new line in the service was needed**. The new counting logic lives in its own small, low-complexity method, and the mapper change is a single guard clause. The database files carry only mechanical column additions (no functions).\n\nFull suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
